1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,089
You edit a file. You save it. Your editor
shows the new line, your local test

2
00:00:09,129 --> 00:00:13,958
passes, and the build server, the shared
machine that builds and tests the team's

3
00:00:13,998 --> 00:00:16,302
code, fails as if you had done nothing.

4
00:00:16,642 --> 00:00:21,648
Here's the fact behind that failure. A
build server checks out a commit, one saved

5
00:00:21,688 --> 00:00:26,571
snapshot of the project. It never sees
your working tree, and it never sees your

6
00:00:26,611 --> 00:00:31,371
index. Both get defined in a minute. For
now: a change that was edited but not

7
00:00:31,411 --> 00:00:36,356
added, or added but not committed, doesn't
exist for anyone else, however real it

8
00:00:36,396 --> 00:00:37,648
looks in your editor.

9
00:00:39,988 --> 00:00:44,764
So there are places a change can be,
and "saved" is only the first of them.

10
00:00:44,804 --> 00:00:49,966
In this video you watch one file travel
through all of them, and after every step

11
00:00:50,006 --> 00:00:53,370
you look inside the dot git
folder to see what moved.

12
00:00:53,410 --> 00:00:56,132
Keep that red build in
mind. It comes back.

13
00:00:57,972 --> 00:01:01,654
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:01:01,694 --> 00:01:06,170
Most people learn git init, git add
and git commit as three words to type.

15
00:01:06,210 --> 00:01:10,442
You're going to learn them as three
sets of files that appear on disk.

16
00:01:10,782 --> 00:01:14,962
We create a repository for a small
evaluation harness called rag-eval.

17
00:01:15,002 --> 00:01:19,121
A repository is the place where a
project's recorded history is kept.

18
00:01:19,161 --> 00:01:22,557
After every step we list the
files in the dot git folder.

19
00:01:22,597 --> 00:01:27,078
The listings leave out the sample hooks,
example scripts that never change.

20
00:01:27,418 --> 00:01:32,360
Nothing here is hidden. The dot git folder
is a directory of ordinary files, and by

21
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:37,042
the end of this video you'll have seen
every file that a first commit creates.

22
00:01:37,082 --> 00:01:41,964
We won't open the objects themselves today.
That's video 7, which starts from this

23
00:01:42,004 --> 00:01:42,684
same commit.

24
00:01:44,524 --> 00:01:46,103
Here's what you'll walk away with.

25
00:01:46,443 --> 00:01:47,855
After this video you can:

26
00:01:48,195 --> 00:01:52,930
Predict which files appear under .git after
git init, after git add and after git

27
00:01:52,970 --> 00:01:53,343
commit.

28
00:01:53,683 --> 00:01:57,494
Read the output of git status in the
four states of the guided repository.

29
00:01:57,834 --> 00:02:02,082
Show that a commit moved the branch ref
and that HEAD still names the branch.

30
00:02:02,422 --> 00:02:05,552
Count the objects that a first
and a second commit create.

31
00:02:07,392 --> 00:02:12,222
git init creates a directory, not a
connection. Recall the last video: the whole

32
00:02:12,262 --> 00:02:16,057
repository is the dot git directory
at the top of your project.

33
00:02:16,097 --> 00:02:21,109
git init creates that directory. It contacts
no server, creates no account and asks

34
00:02:21,149 --> 00:02:24,275
no permission, because Git
has none of those things.

35
00:02:24,615 --> 00:02:29,384
One file, three places. A file you work
on can be in three places, and the three

36
00:02:29,424 --> 00:02:32,028
commands of this video
move it between them.

37
00:02:32,368 --> 00:02:36,190
The first place is the working
tree: the files you see and edit.

38
00:02:36,230 --> 00:02:39,328
Editing files is invisible
to Git until you tell it.

39
00:02:39,668 --> 00:02:43,322
The second place is the index,
also called the staging area.

40
00:02:43,362 --> 00:02:48,493
It's one file, named index, inside the dot
git folder, and it's the list of what the

41
00:02:48,533 --> 00:02:50,032
next commit will contain.

42
00:02:50,372 --> 00:02:55,420
The third place is the repository proper:
the objects in the objects folder and the

43
00:02:55,460 --> 00:03:00,324
names in the refs folder, both inside dot
git. On screen the third box shows the

44
00:03:00,364 --> 00:03:03,757
last commit, which Git finds
through a name called HEAD.

45
00:03:04,097 --> 00:03:08,935
What git add writes. git add does two
things. It stores the content of the file as

46
00:03:08,975 --> 00:03:13,575
an object, one stored piece of data. And
it records in the index which content

47
00:03:13,615 --> 00:03:16,787
belongs to which path. No
commit exists after git add.

48
00:03:17,127 --> 00:03:20,167
What git commit writes. git
commit records the index.

49
00:03:20,207 --> 00:03:24,874
It writes the objects that describe the
directories, called trees, and one commit

50
00:03:24,914 --> 00:03:29,754
object. Then it moves the current branch to
that commit and writes a line in each of

51
00:03:29,794 --> 00:03:31,265
two journals, the reflogs.

52
00:03:31,605 --> 00:03:36,325
The mechanism of "being on a branch". A
branch is a name that points at a commit.

53
00:03:36,365 --> 00:03:39,322
HEAD names a branch, and
the branch names a commit.

54
00:03:39,362 --> 00:03:44,258
That's the whole mechanism. A commit moves
the current branch, and HEAD follows only

55
00:03:44,298 --> 00:03:45,962
because it names that branch.

56
00:03:46,302 --> 00:03:51,005
The risk labels. All three commands
carry the label 🟢 SAFE in the textbook's

57
00:03:51,045 --> 00:03:55,810
command-safety table. git init creates
the dot git folder, and in an existing

58
00:03:55,850 --> 00:04:00,865
repository it only adds missing template
files. git add stores content as objects

59
00:04:00,905 --> 00:04:05,983
and points index entries at it. git commit
adds tree and commit objects, moves the

60
00:04:06,023 --> 00:04:09,914
current branch, and appends to
the reflogs. Each one only adds.

61
00:04:11,754 --> 00:04:15,881
Picture three boxes in a row:
working tree, index, repository.

62
00:04:15,921 --> 00:04:20,989
A file starts in the left box. git add
copies its content to the middle box.

63
00:04:21,029 --> 00:04:24,551
git commit records the middle
box into the right box.

64
00:04:24,891 --> 00:04:31,123
Notice the word "copies". After git add,
the content is in the working tree and in

65
00:04:31,163 --> 00:04:37,472
the index. After git commit, it's in all
three. Nothing leaves a box when it enters

66
00:04:37,512 --> 00:04:38,466
the next one.

67
00:04:38,806 --> 00:04:43,584
Where does this model stop being enough?
It tells you where content is, and it says

68
00:04:43,624 --> 00:04:48,459
nothing yet about how the right-hand box is
organized: what a commit is made of, and

69
00:04:48,499 --> 00:04:51,825
why a second commit doesn't
store an unchanged file again.

70
00:04:51,865 --> 00:04:54,844
You'll see that the object
count hints at an answer.

71
00:04:54,884 --> 00:04:56,353
The full answer is Part 1.

72
00:05:03,993 --> 00:05:07,365
Build it from the left. The
working tree holds two files.

73
00:05:07,405 --> 00:05:11,436
The index lists the same two paths,
each with the ID of its content.

74
00:05:11,476 --> 00:05:16,345
The repository holds two commits, and the
arrow between them points from the newer

75
00:05:16,385 --> 00:05:21,255
commit back to the older one. refs/heads/main
points at the newer commit, and HEAD

76
00:05:21,295 --> 00:05:26,164
says main. The two arrows underneath are
the two commands: git add carries content

77
00:05:26,204 --> 00:05:30,654
from the working tree to the index, and
git commit records the index in the

78
00:05:30,694 --> 00:05:31,312
repository.

79
00:05:33,152 --> 00:05:34,496
Replay it yourself with:

80
00:05:34,836 --> 00:05:40,461
The IDs on my screen equal the IDs in
section 1.9 of the book, because replays use

81
00:05:40,501 --> 00:05:41,566
the fixed clock.

82
00:05:41,906 --> 00:05:43,999
Step 1: git init 🟢 SAFE.

83
00:05:44,339 --> 00:05:48,721
Predict: after git init, does a
branch exist? Say it out loud.

84
00:05:48,761 --> 00:05:49,434
I'll wait.

85
00:05:54,274 --> 00:05:57,760
git init created the directory
and, inside it, .git.

86
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:03,321
Three entries matter now. objects/ will
hold every version of every file, and it's

87
00:06:03,361 --> 00:06:07,661
empty. refs/ will hold branch and
tag names, and it's empty too.

88
00:06:07,701 --> 00:06:13,086
HEAD says which branch you're on:
refs/heads/main. No such file exists yet under

89
00:06:13,126 --> 00:06:16,681
refs/, and that's why git
status says No commits yet.

90
00:06:16,721 --> 00:06:21,970
So: not yet. Git calls this an unborn
branch. The name main comes from the lab

91
00:06:22,010 --> 00:06:22,919
configuration.

92
00:06:23,259 --> 00:06:27,300
That's the first of four git status
states: no commits, nothing to commit.

93
00:06:27,640 --> 00:06:29,711
Step 2: create files.

94
00:06:30,051 --> 00:06:34,052
The script creates README.md
and configs/eval.yaml.

95
00:06:34,092 --> 00:06:36,666
Predict: what changes under .git?

96
00:06:37,006 --> 00:06:40,837
Nothing changed. .git holds
the same four files as before.

97
00:06:40,877 --> 00:06:45,707
Two files exist in the working tree,
and git status calls them untracked.

98
00:06:45,747 --> 00:06:51,246
Notice that it shows configs/ as one line,
because nothing inside that directory is

99
00:06:51,286 --> 00:06:54,315
tracked. Second status
state: untracked files.

100
00:06:54,655 --> 00:07:00,462
Try it now, on paper. Draw the three
boxes: working tree, index, repository.

101
00:07:00,502 --> 00:07:06,540
Put a mark for configs/eval.yaml in every
box that holds its content right now.

102
00:07:06,580 --> 00:07:09,541
Thirty seconds, then check your answer.

103
00:07:14,381 --> 00:07:18,622
One mark, in the left box. The file
is saved, and Git hasn't been told.

104
00:07:18,662 --> 00:07:21,336
If you marked more,
that's the natural guess.

105
00:07:21,676 --> 00:07:23,600
Step 3: git add 🟢 SAFE.

106
00:07:23,940 --> 00:07:28,892
Now the question I asked you to hold: what
will change under .git when we add the

107
00:07:28,932 --> 00:07:31,419
two files? How many new files, and where?

108
00:07:33,759 --> 00:07:40,034
Three files are new. .git/index is the
index. The two files under objects/ hold the

109
00:07:40,074 --> 00:07:46,350
contents of README.md and configs/eval.yaml.
Each object is named by a 40-digit ID,

110
00:07:46,390 --> 00:07:50,764
split into a two-digit directory
and a 38-digit file name.

111
00:07:50,804 --> 00:07:54,416
Read the first one:
directory 3a, then the rest.

112
00:07:54,756 --> 00:08:01,039
git ls-files --stage prints the index:
file mode, object ID, stage number, path.

113
00:08:01,079 --> 00:08:06,572
Look at the IDs: 3a79082 for README.md,
422e9c0 for configs/eval.yaml.

114
00:08:06,612 --> 00:08:12,737
They're the names of the two object files
above. So git add did two things: it

115
00:08:12,777 --> 00:08:18,428
stored the content, and it recorded
which content belongs to which path.

116
00:08:18,768 --> 00:08:23,504
Third status state: "Changes to be
committed". And still No commits yet.

117
00:08:23,844 --> 00:08:25,794
Step 4: git commit 🟢 SAFE.

118
00:08:26,134 --> 00:08:31,175
Same question. What will change under .git?
Think about objects, about refs/, and

119
00:08:31,215 --> 00:08:32,429
about anything else.

120
00:08:34,769 --> 00:08:39,625
Read the first line of the output:
[main (root-commit) f7c044e].

121
00:08:39,665 --> 00:08:45,134
On branch main, a commit without a
parent, whose ID starts with f7c044e.

122
00:08:45,474 --> 00:08:50,410
Seven files are new. Three are
objects: the commit, in directory f7.

123
00:08:50,450 --> 00:08:55,387
A tree for the top directory, in
e6. And a tree for configs/, in 0d.

124
00:08:55,427 --> 00:09:01,023
A tree is Git's record of one directory.
Then refs/heads/main: the branch now

125
00:09:01,063 --> 00:09:07,170
exists. Then logs/HEAD and logs/refs/heads/main:
the reflogs, journals of where HEAD

126
00:09:07,210 --> 00:09:12,952
and main have pointed. And COMMIT_EDITMSG:
the text of the last commit message.

127
00:09:13,292 --> 00:09:17,983
HEAD still contains the branch name.
The branch file contains the commit ID.

128
00:09:18,023 --> 00:09:22,651
git status compares that commit, the
index and the working tree, finds them

129
00:09:22,691 --> 00:09:26,697
identical, and reports a clean
working tree. Fourth status state.

130
00:09:27,037 --> 00:09:29,046
Step 5: a second commit.

131
00:09:29,386 --> 00:09:35,366
The script appends one line to configs/eval.yaml.
Before it stages anything, look at

132
00:09:35,406 --> 00:09:37,086
git status and git diff.

133
00:09:40,426 --> 00:09:45,999
After the edit, git status lists the file
under "Changes not staged for commit": the

134
00:09:46,039 --> 00:09:51,345
working tree differs from the index, and
git diff shows that difference, the one

135
00:09:51,385 --> 00:09:56,825
added line top_k: 5. git add copies the
new content into the index, and git commit

136
00:09:56,865 --> 00:10:01,770
records the index. The log now has two
commits, 2da8d74 on top of f7c044e.

137
00:10:02,110 --> 00:10:06,684
Here's that step in the three boxes.
The edit changes only the working tree.

138
00:10:06,724 --> 00:10:11,723
The add copies it into the index. The commit
records it, and all three agree again.

139
00:10:12,063 --> 00:10:17,607
Now the prediction that this whole video
was built for, as a quiz with two options.

140
00:10:17,647 --> 00:10:22,316
HEAD is a file. refs/heads/main is
a file. We committed a second time.

141
00:10:22,356 --> 00:10:26,487
Option one: HEAD changed. Option
two: the branch file changed.

142
00:10:26,527 --> 00:10:28,304
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

143
00:10:33,144 --> 00:10:38,437
Option two. HEAD did not change. The
branch file did: it now holds the ID that

144
00:10:38,477 --> 00:10:44,180
begins 2da8d74. In the picture the label
main moved to the new commit, and HEAD went

145
00:10:44,220 --> 00:10:49,717
with it. The file HEAD still names main,
because HEAD follows the branch by name.

146
00:10:49,757 --> 00:10:54,640
logs/HEAD has one line per movement:
old ID, new ID, who, when, and why.

147
00:10:54,680 --> 00:10:58,605
The first old ID is all zeros
because nothing came before.

148
00:10:58,945 --> 00:11:04,249
And the count: nine objects. Five from the
first commit and four from the second,

149
00:11:04,289 --> 00:11:09,593
namely one new file content, two new trees
because configs/ and the top directory

150
00:11:09,633 --> 00:11:14,145
both changed, and the commit. README.md
was not stored a second time.

151
00:11:14,185 --> 00:11:16,982
Keep that observation.
Video 7 explains it.

152
00:11:17,322 --> 00:11:22,252
As a picture: two commits. The label main
points at the newer one, and HEAD names

153
00:11:22,292 --> 00:11:22,558
main.

154
00:11:22,898 --> 00:11:28,568
One more replay, to read an empty .git in
full. This is the first part of Lab 0.2.

155
00:11:37,408 --> 00:11:42,222
This time nothing is left out: the
directories, then every file, including the

156
00:11:42,262 --> 00:11:43,031
sample hooks.

157
00:11:43,371 --> 00:11:46,983
Read them out loud. HEAD: one
line, the name of a branch.

158
00:11:47,023 --> 00:11:51,275
config: the repository's own settings.
description: a line of text.

159
00:11:51,315 --> 00:11:56,656
info/exclude: comments only. And a sample
hook, which says in its own comment how it

160
00:11:56,696 --> 00:12:01,845
would be enabled. I stop the replay here.
The rest of the lab removes these files

161
00:12:01,885 --> 00:12:06,329
one group at a time, to find out
which of them Git can't live without.

162
00:12:06,369 --> 00:12:07,931
That experiment is yours.

163
00:12:12,771 --> 00:12:14,419
Five mistakes to watch for.

164
00:12:14,759 --> 00:12:18,710
git commit records nothing, or
records a commit that lacks a file.

165
00:12:18,750 --> 00:12:23,486
Root cause: git commit records the index,
and the change was never added to it.

166
00:12:23,826 --> 00:12:27,097
Believing that saving a file
changes the repository.

167
00:12:27,137 --> 00:12:32,445
Root cause: editing files is invisible to
Git until git add; .git held the same four

168
00:12:32,485 --> 00:12:34,482
files before and after the edit.

169
00:12:34,822 --> 00:12:38,084
Expecting HEAD to hold the
new commit ID after a commit.

170
00:12:38,124 --> 00:12:42,625
Root cause: HEAD names the branch; the
commit moves the branch file, and HEAD

171
00:12:42,665 --> 00:12:44,453
follows only through that name.

172
00:12:44,793 --> 00:12:49,890
Running git init in the wrong place. Root
cause: git init creates .git wherever it

173
00:12:49,930 --> 00:12:55,028
runs; in your home directory it turns
everything you own into one working tree, so

174
00:12:55,068 --> 00:12:56,845
give it a directory argument.

175
00:12:57,185 --> 00:13:02,649
Expecting your hand-typed IDs to match the
book. Root cause: in labs/shell the clock

176
00:13:02,689 --> 00:13:05,728
is real, and the commit
ID depends on the time.

177
00:13:07,568 --> 00:13:12,581
Now, out of the lab. A backend engineer
changes a retry limit in two places: a

178
00:13:12,621 --> 00:13:17,828
configuration file and a constant in the
code. She stages the configuration file,

179
00:13:17,868 --> 00:13:23,140
commits, and pushes. Locally everything
passes, because her test reads the working

180
00:13:23,180 --> 00:13:28,582
tree, where both edits exist. The build
server checks out the commit, where only one

181
00:13:28,622 --> 00:13:29,036
exists.

182
00:13:29,376 --> 00:13:34,397
Her report says "the fix is committed", and
by the meaning of the three boxes that's

183
00:13:34,437 --> 00:13:39,037
half true: one file reached the right-hand
box, and the other is still in the

184
00:13:39,077 --> 00:13:42,954
left-hand one. That's the red build
from the start of this video.

185
00:13:42,994 --> 00:13:45,967
Video 6 works this case
from report to prevention.

186
